ent. The question specifically asks to use the 1953 CPI, but we'd need both to convert between years.\n\nActually, thinking about this more carefully:\n- If we're adjusting to 1953 dollars: 1940_amount_1953 = 1940_amount * (CPI_1953 / CPI_1940)\n\n\n- If we're adjusting to 1940 dollars: 1953_amount_1940 = 1953_amount * (CPI_1940 / CPI_1953)\n\nI'll verify the inflation adjustment calculation. The core requirement is converting expenditures to a consistent monetary value using the CPI-U. This means finding the precise annual average CPI for both 1940 and 1953 to ensure accurate inflation adjustment. The key is using the correct conversion factor that reflects the purchasing power difference between these two years.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_c20e3d0ece9a4c02a36d3a50","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://www.minneapolisfed.org/about-us/monitoring-the-economy/consumer-price-index/consumer-price-index-1913-to-present\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"1940|1953\" | head -10"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
